A||||13||myblack86GT||Installing New Front Speakers||||8||
Z||000000||myblack86GT||09-09-2000||05:53 PM||||I ordered some 6x9's online, got them in today, and tried installing them. I started on the Pass. side. I took the old speaker out and the subbox out. These by the way are Pioneer 4ways. I read on some other posted topics to use a sharp utility knife to cut the dash. I tried this about and hour ago and havent got anywhere. I need some help, iv read the threads on here, but I can quite seem to do it right. How do I get the speaker in there? I tried to put it through the bottem but the air duct is in the way. And I havent ever tried the drivers side yet. Thanks for the help. Also, someone named Seanh on here? I read some of his posts and he seems to have a nice system in his car with these same front speakers. How do I do this? ||||reg||8
Z||000001||myblack86GT||09-09-2000||10:01 PM||||Nevermind, Seanh emailed me with some info. I also just decided to take the dash out. Im almost done taking it out, Im suprised, it reallt wasnt that time comsuming or difficult.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-09-2000||10:04 PM||||I took my dash out to install my 6x9s. I think it was Gokart who said he was able to install them on both sides of the dash without removing it. That was impossible with the 6x9s I bought, so I guess it depends on brand of speaker. The BA's that I bought and installed are almost too deep for the driver side cavity. It rests right on the frame below the dash. <P>Here is what I had to do to remove dash:<BR>-remove instrument cluster<BR>-remove rally guages<BR>-remove radio surround, faceplate<BR>-remove shifter surround<BR>-disconnect fuse block from dash<BR>-disconnect hood release handle from dash<BR>-remove 6 bolts/screws that hold dash in place<BR>-disconnect speakers<P>I think thats all, may have forgotten a couple items. Expect it to take about an hour the first time you remove the dash.||||reg||1
Z||000003||myblack86GT||09-10-2000||09:24 PM||||I just got done with this job. Got the new Speakers in, and WOW. They blow away any 4x10 iv ever heard. I also didnt have enough room on the drivers side either, but it barly fits in there. I am so impressed with the sound. There Grrrrrrrrrreat. I would highly reccomend do the 6x9 upgrade to anyone thinking about it. Thanks for the help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||09-10-2000||10:11 PM||||They really do make a difference! Even if the installation does looks "crappy" UNDER the grill! I know they sound BETTER <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif"> <P>Marc                     87GT||||reg||12
Z||000005||Black88GT||09-10-2000||11:38 PM||||I don't know about 6x9s in the dash.  I for one like the bass to come from behind me and more treble from up front.  Also, it would seem the dash would rattle like crazy since it wasn't really designed for that type of bass (don't even mention that poor excuse for a "subwoofer").  I guess you could always put some of that Dynomat stuff on it.  <P>Oh, and taking the dash out is a pain in the butt!  I have only done it with a junkyard car and it got to the point where I was just ripping stuff out of it!  I couldn't imagine doing it on a car I actually cared about.<P><BR> <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000006||-=shame=-||09-11-2000||07:58 AM||||So, what is better? Pry that little faded screen off or tear everything out and go at it from underneath? Plus I wouldn't mind replacing that fabric so that it matched my dash.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||09-11-2000||08:31 AM||||I believe you'll have to pry the screen off either way.<P>BTW you can re-color the fabric on the speaker grills.  Mr. Mike's suggested using the SEM spray using the SEM procedure for dyeing fabric.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Blade||09-11-2000||02:20 PM||||anyone have a step by step for this conversion id like to do it sometime.<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||09-11-2000||03:06 PM||||I just recovered my dash speaker grills today. I used some black speaker fabric I bought at RadioShack, and some spray on glue I bought from AutoZone. Total cost is about $16, but I have much more than needed. Takes about 30 minutes to do the pair. Trimming takes most of the time. Very recommended. Pictures are still coming. Will be posted later.||||reg||1
Z||000010||myblack86GT||09-11-2000||05:30 PM||||Actually, Your bass should be comming from the front stage, not behind you, but I guess its personal preference. I really didnt find it that hard to take off the dash. Its not somthing id want to do again, but its somthing that was well worth the product. The drivers side speaker fits really thight, and I cant put the subwoofer port tube in because of the new speaker on the pass. side, But honestly, these new speakers put out better quality bass then that factory sub, but that sub does add alot to the factory setup. I dont find I have any trouble with the dash rattleing, I used wire ties to hook the speakers in there, and they are very stable and dont move. Some speakers require that you have to take the Fiero dash out, and some will fit in the top, but I think I am alot happier knowing that it is all secured and fitted correctly before I put the dash in the car, so IMO, I would take the dash out. Blade, email Seanh at sean@kdsi.net  he helped me out alot, he should be able to tell you step by step the process of taking the dash out and re-installation. Ill post some Pictures of the the speakers once I get them developed, I really need a digital cam.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Lunapark||09-11-2000||10:25 PM||||I guess if you like SPL bass coming from the back is a good thing. Otherwise, bass should come from the front just like everything else. That is the way it was recorded. Right?||||reg||1
Z||000012||myblack86GT||09-11-2000||10:55 PM||||Thats right. Sean told me about his bass is his system, and showed me a website that sell a subbox that sits upfront of the car, removing the spare tire. He says it sounds great, but Im not ready to spend this kinda money just to make my car sound good for the 20 min. drive to work and back home. I wish he would post some info, IT WOULD BE HELPFUL hint hint). Those 6x9's make such a huge improvment. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||09-12-2000||02:55 PM||||Recoloring speaker grills<P>If I recall, the SEM procedure is:<P>Clean the fabric before re-coloring.<BR>Spray a small area of fabric with a light coat of the SEM spray.  Immediately brush it with a nylon bristle brush (like a tooth brush) to keep the the wet spray from caking up the fabric fibers.<P>I called the SEM folks a while back and they sent me a ring binder full of product info and how-to procedures.<P>As I mentioned before their Light Grey is a perfect match for the 85 grey interior.  The graphite Grey is what Mr. Mike's recommends for the grey speaker grills.  I got both at a local parts store.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
